Z||000005||thomas_l||05-29-2001||09:04 AM||||Exchange is usually around 67¢US = $1CD. Price sounds a little on the high side, but that's just me (cheap  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">). In my shopping, it seemed like the mid-miles GT's were going for $2000 - 2500 in fair, running, daily driveable condition. Assuming your assessment doesn't leave any surprises, I wouldn't give more than $3000CD, and that's if everything else is perfect. I mean, after the purchase, you still have some work to do and money to spend. Make sure you leave yourself a little breathing room.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Voytek||05-29-2001||02:51 PM||||If you can get him down to about $4000 - $4200 it's not a bad deal (as long as the car is in a decent condition and that's the original mileage).  The clutch can be a big expense - ask him to show you a receipt and if he can't, ask where it was done.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Ken Wittlief||05-29-2001||03:08 PM||||carfax cant help you with the canadian VIN number - they cant get canadian records.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Ken Wittlief||05-29-2001||03:13 PM||||It sounds like the owner knows the car and likes it, and is parting with it reluctantly - so if you buy his car its gonna be on the high side.<P>  also if he ate the clutch with 80,000 miles on it, he ran the car somewhat hard - if you treat a clutch nice it can last 160,000 miles or more.<P>  to get the best deal you need to find someone who has one that needs some work, which they cant do, or someone that hates their car and wants to get rid of it.<P>  but that is a risk, because the car may have given them good reason to be hated.||||reg||1
